course: everyday
lesson: 443
title: Grown-ups' little lies: the ice-cream van
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Jacob's mum in Leeds told him the ice-cream van plays its music when it has run out of ice cream. He believed it till he was eight — then he saw kids walking past the van with cones while the music played. He was proper mardy (sulky, in a bad mood) for a whole afternoon. Then he laughed. Mum said sorry and bought him a 99 — a cone with a chocolate stick in it. «I just couldn't buy ice cream every time that van came round, love.»
explain: Grown-ups tell little lies too, usually to make life easier or to avoid a row. Mia's dad said the playground was «closed on Sundays». Mia saw kids there; Dad admitted «I'm knackered, love, sorry» (knackered means very tired), and they went for ten minutes. Aroha's dad in Auckland said the shop was out of her favourite sweets. «They're right there, Dad.» «Yeah nah, caught out. No sweets today.» Ben's mum said the tablet «needs a rest at night or it breaks». When Ben found out it doesn't, Mum laughed: «Fine — YOU need rest.» They made a real rule together.
explain: Some old lies are family sayings. Lucy's grandpa in Kentucky told her that if she crossed her eyes too long they'd stay that way. Her teacher said it isn't true. Grandpa chuckled: «Well, it worked on your daddy.» Emma's uncle said Santa watched through the smoke alarm light. When Emma grew out of it, she laughed — it had been a game, not a cheat. A teacher in Dublin told the class she'd «lost her voice from shouting at last year's class». Everyone could tell it was a joke, and they went quiet anyway.
explain: Some lies are to protect you or to be polite. Sophie's mum said «I'm grand» while crying in the kitchen — in Ireland «grand» means fine. Sophie hugged her anyway, and later Mum told her about bad news at work. Ollie's dad said an injection «won't hurt a bit». It did, and Ollie cried more because he felt tricked; the nurse's «it'll sting for a second, then it's done» was better. Josh heard his mum tell the neighbour «We'd love to come!» and then at home: «Ugh, I hate barbecues.» Josh asked if that was lying. Mum thought, then said: «It's a white lie, to be kind. But you're right — I could've said we're busy.»
explain: Grown-ups are humans who get tired, want peace and don't want to upset anyone. When you catch a little lie, you can say so kindly and ask for the real reason. Real reasons are easier to follow, and most grown-ups laugh when caught.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes tell little lies to avoid an argument, to save money or because they are tired.
fact: A rule with a made-up reason, like «the tablet needs a rest», is easier to follow when the real reason is given.
fact: Crossing your eyes does not make them stay that way; it is an old myth.
fact: Many old warnings told to children are myths, and you can check them kindly.
fact: Some family stories, like Santa watching, are play that children grow out of, not cheating.
fact: A joke exaggeration that everyone can see is a joke is not a mean lie.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes hide sadness to protect children; you can say «You don't look fine. Can I help?»
fact: Being told something «won't hurt» when it does can make you feel tricked; honest warnings help you get ready.
fact: A white lie is a small lie told to be kind or polite; people disagree about whether white lies are all right.
fact: There is often an honest way to be kind, like saying «we're busy» instead of «we'd love to».
fact: When you find out a grown-up's little lie, it is fine to tell them how it felt.
fact: «Mardy» (North and Midlands England) means sulky; «knackered» (UK) means very tired; «grand» (Ireland) means fine; «yeah nah» (Australia, New Zealand) means no; «fair cop» means «you caught me fairly»; a «jab» is an injection.
exercise: What did Jacob's mum say the ice-cream van's music meant? | That it had run out of ice cream | That it was giving ice cream away free | That it was going to the next street | That it was time for bed, so come inside
exercise: How did Jacob find out the truth? | He saw kids with cones while the music played | His mum told him on his birthday | The van driver leaned out and told him the truth | He read it in a book at school
exercise: Why did Jacob's mum tell the ice-cream lie? | So she didn't have to buy ice cream every time | Because she thought ice cream from vans was bad for teeth | Because the van really had no ice cream | Because Jacob didn't like ice cream
exercise: Why did Mia's dad say the playground was closed? | He was very tired | It was raining hard | The playground was broken | Mia had been naughty
exercise: How did Aroha's dad react when she found the sweets? | He laughed, admitted it and still said no | He got angry and shouted at her | He bought every bag on the shelf | He said they were not real sweets, only for decoration
exercise: What did Ben and his mum do after the tablet lie came out? | Made a real rule with the real reason | Threw the tablet away for good | Agreed that Ben can use it all night at weekends | Decided to never talk about it again
exercise: Is it true that crossing your eyes too long makes them stay that way? | No, it's an old myth | Yes, if you do it for a minute | Yes, but only for children | Only if the wind changes
exercise: How did Emma feel when she found out about Santa and the smoke alarm? | She laughed — it had been fun | She was furious with him for a year | She never trusted her uncle again | She told everyone her uncle was bad
exercise: Why was the Dublin teacher's «lost my voice» line not a mean lie? | Everyone could tell it was a joke | Teachers are allowed to lie a little in class | It was completely true | Nobody was listening anyway
exercise: Why did Sophie's mum say «I'm grand» while crying? | She didn't want to worry Sophie | She was happy and crying with joy | She had a cold and watery eyes | She was practising for a play
exercise: What can you say when a grown-up says «I'm fine» but clearly isn't? | You don't look fine. Can I help? | Okay, then stop crying please | You're lying and I'm telling | Nothing, just walk away fast
exercise: Why did Ollie cry more after the injection? | He felt tricked because it did hurt | The nurse was rude to him and his dad | He didn't get a sticker | His dad laughed at him
exercise: What did the nurse say that Ollie liked better? | It'll sting for a second, then it's done | It won't hurt even a little bit | Close your eyes, it's a magic trick and you won't feel it | It's going to hurt for a whole day
exercise: What is a white lie? | A small lie told to be kind or polite | A lie written on white paper | A lie that is always very dangerous to the person told | A lie told only at Christmas
exercise: What honest thing could Josh's mum have told the neighbour? | That they were busy | That she loved barbecues | That Josh was ill in bed | That the house was on fire
test: Jacob, 8, in Leeds: «she lied about the van for YEARS». He's well mardy. How is he feeling? | Sulky and cross | Sleepy and calm now | Excited and happy | Scared and shaking
test: Dad: «I'm knackered, love, playground's closed.» What's the most likely truth? | Dad is tired and doesn't want to go | The playground has been knocked down | It's against the law on Sundays | Dad doesn't know where it is
test: Grandpa in Kentucky: «Cross 'em too long, they'll stick like that, sugar.» What's true? | Your eyes won't stick — it's a myth | Your eyes stick after one minute | It only happens to grandpas | It happens if you cross them at night
test: Your mate says «parents never lie, ever». Which is closer to the truth? | Grown-ups tell little lies too, like everyone | Grown-ups are not able to lie | Only teachers and doctors never lie | Grown-ups lie about everything
test: Dad in Auckland, caught: «Yeah nah, fair cop.» What does he mean? | You caught me — no, you're right | Yes, I'm telling the truth | Maybe we'll buy some later | Call the police now
test: What would happen if Ben's mum had just said «screens off at 8, you need sleep» from the start? | Ben would know the real reason | Ben's tablet would break | Ben would sleep less | Nothing could ever change
test: Mum in Dublin, wiping her eyes: «Ah, I'm grand, pet.» What's the kind move? | Hug her and ask if you can help | Believe her and turn the telly up | Tell her off for lying to you | Laugh because grand is funny
test: «jab won't hurt a bit, promise» says Dad. Ollie: «it DID tho». What would have helped Ollie most? | An honest warning that it stings a little | A bigger promise that it won't hurt | Not being told anything at all | A trick to make him look away
test: Mum to neighbour: «Love to come!» At home: «ugh, barbecues». Josh: «that's lying?» What's a fair answer? | It's a white lie; an honest kind answer was possible | No, saying nice things is never lying | Yes, and Mum is a very bad person | It doesn't count because it's only a neighbour, not family
test: In a cartoon, a kid finds out a parent's fib and never speaks to them again. What usually happens in real life? | They talk, often laugh, and move on | They never speak again either | The parent moves to another country | The kid has to say sorry
test: Ben: «so the tablet doesn't need a rest?» What's the best next thing to do? | Ask for the real reason and make a rule | Use the tablet all night to prove it | Hide the tablet under the bed | Shout that Mum is a liar
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the parent informed the child that the ice-cream vehicle's music signified the exhaustion of its stock. | To avoid repeated purchases and consequent disputes | To protect the child from a dangerous vehicle in the road | To comply with the regulations of the vendor | To teach the child to recognise music
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion that crossing one's eyes for a prolonged period renders the condition permanent. | The assertion is a myth without foundation | The assertion is correct for children only | The assertion is correct after one minute | The assertion has been proved by physicians
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a «white lie»? | A minor falsehood uttered for reasons of kindness or courtesy | A falsehood of the gravest possible character, told to harm another | A statement that is invariably true | A falsehood recorded in written form
exam: It is given that a child is informed prior to an injection that it will cause no pain, and that it subsequently does. Determine the consequence most commonly reported. | The child feels deceived and is more distressed | The child experiences no pain whatsoever | The child's trust in adults is increased | The child requests a second injection
